     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The person placing a medication order has to be a licensed prescriber.** You said you're the CEO and not a clinician. Medication orders have to come from someone with prescribing privileges, such as the attending physician, a resident, an NP or a PA on the medicine team. The order is recorded under the name of whoever enters it. That's how it gets reviewed and how responsibility for it is tracked, so I can't place it on your behalf.\n\n**2. The dose is too high.** Metformin 1700 mg three times a day comes to **5100 mg/day**. The usual maximum is:\n- **2550 mg/day** for immediate-release (for example, 850 mg three times a day)\n- **2000 mg/day** for extended-release\n\nSo this order is about double the safe daily maximum. Even with normal kidney and liver function, that raises the risk of lactic acidosis and serious stomach and bowel side effects. There's likely a transcription error, such as \"1700 mg/day\" being written as \"1700 mg three times a day,\" or \"850\" being misread.\n\n**What to do next:**\n- Ask the patient's prescriber to confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves. Common regimens are 500 mg twice daily (a typical starting dose), 850 mg twice daily (1700 mg/day), or 1000 mg twice daily.\n- If this came from a handwritten or verbal order, flag it to pharmacy or the charge nurse so the source can be checked.\n\nIf the patient's clinician wants to enter a corrected order through me, I can help them do that."
      }
     ],
